new(134,"PED:US-CCSS-MATH:G2:U09:L01","https://im.kendallhunt.com/k5/teachers/grade-2/unit-9/lesson-1/preparation.html","3a39adaea697e812889cb50b04fd27cb13e85d426bb649fe55080732731229d9",new string[]{"CCSS:2.OA.B.2"},new string[]{"Learning goal: You will fluently add and subtract within 20 using mental strategies.\nIn this lesson, you find the value of sums and unknown addends from memory in expressions with values within 20. In the first activity, you complete an inventory of sums within 20 and identify those sums that you do not know yet from memory. In the second activity, you continue to practice fluency with addition and subtraction within 20.","Key ideas:\n- Fluently add and subtract within 20\n- In this lesson, you find the value of sums and unknown addends from memory in expressions with values within 20\n- Subtraction can represent taking away, comparing quantities, or finding a missing addend; the representation must match the situation.","Example 1: Find the value of each expression mentally. \\(10 - 5\\) \\(11 - 5\\) \\(12 - 6\\) \\(13 - 6\\)\nApproach: Represent the whole and the part, write a matching subtraction relationship, then find and check the difference.\nExample 2: Find the value of each sum mentally. \\(6 + 6\\) \\(9 + 9\\) \\(7 + 7\\) \\(8 + 8\\) \\(5 + 5\\) \\(9 + 8\\) \\(7 + 8\\) \\(8 + 5\\) \\(6 + 5\\) \\(7 + 6\\) \\(7 + 9\\) \\(6 + 8\\) \\(9 + 2\\) \\(7 + 5\\) \\(8 + 4\\) \\(2 + 9\\) \\(9 + 7\\) \\(6 + 9\\) \\(8 + 3\\) \\(9 + 4\\) \\(8 + 6\\) \\(3 + 9\\) \\(5 + 6\\) \\(9 + 5\\) \\(5 + 9\\) \\(9 + 6\\) \\(8 + 9\\) \\(6 + 7\\) \\(5 + 7\\) \\(8 + 7\\) \\(12 - 6...\nApproach: Represent the whole and the part, write a matching subtraction relationship, then find and check the difference.","Step 1: Identify the whole and the part being removed or compared.\nStep 2: Represent the situation with objects, a drawing, number line, or equation.\nStep 3: Write a subtraction relationship that matches the representation.\nStep 4: Find the difference using an efficient strategy.\nStep 5: Check the result with the original quantities or a related addition fact.","Common mistake: mixing up the whole and the part being removed or compared. Check that the subtraction relationship matches the representation.","You will fluently add and subtract within 20 using mental strategies. In this lesson, you find the value of sums and unknown addends from memory in expressions with values within 20."},new string[]{"IM2021 G2 Unit9 Lesson1: Sums and Differences Within 20. Verified public source-specific tasks and mathematics: Mentally calculate 10−5=5,11−5=6,12−6=6,13−6=7. Original Activity1 Check Yourself: inventory of addition facts within20, students circle personally unknown sums and write THEIR OWN flashcards. Activity2 Spin and Find Missing Number uses two real spinners to select total and known addend. Source media and human assessment boundary: Real chosen card inventory, correct student self-reported difficulty, actual spinner results, partner discussions and inaccessible Cool-down require human; first-party companion never claims original outcomes.","Focus: Mentally calculate 10−5=5,11−5=6,12−6=6,13−6=7. Original Activity1 Check Yourself: inventory of addition facts within20, students circle personally unknown sums and write THEIR OWN flashcards. Activity2 Spin and Find Missing Number uses two real spinners to select total and known addend. Standard mappings provided by ORIGINAL lesson graph: CCSS:2.OA.B.2. Original visual/physical question materials and authored open writing remain distinct from NEW Edulytics first-party numeric examples.","Actual public publisher activity: Mentally calculate 10−5=5,11−5=6,12−6=6,13−6=7. Original Activity1 Check Yourself: inventory of addition facts within20, students circle personally unknown sums and write THEIR OWN flashcards. Activity2 Spin and Find Missing Number uses two real spinners to select total and known addend. Independent first-party mathematical example uses different va