new(136,"PED:US-CCSS-MATH:G2:U09:L03","https://im.kendallhunt.com/k5/teachers/grade-2/unit-9/lesson-3/preparation.html","e67bd2743cc7c9afebe7a6ca4703f99681551351dabe6c0d3d0a120e85a85ac9",new string[]{"CCSS:2.MD.A.1","CCSS:2.MD.A.4","CCSS:2.MD.B.5","CCSS:2.OA.B.2"},new string[]{"Learning goal: You will measure lengths in centimeters and add and subtract lengths within 20 to answer questions.\nIn this lesson, you add and subtract within 20 and build fluency within this range of numbers. You also practice measuring lengths in centimeters and use your measurements to add and subtract. In the first activity, you measure lengths on a map and add and subtract your measurements to answer questions.","Key ideas:\n- Fluently add and subtract within 20\n- Measure lengths in standard units\n- Measurement units must be equal in size and placed or interpreted without gaps, overlaps, or a shifted starting point.","Example 1: Draw a line to represent the distance between cities on the map. Then measure the length in centimeters. Noah started in Trenton, New Jersey. First, he traveled to Harrisburg, Pennsylvania. ___ cm Next, he traveled from Harrisburg to Indianapolis, Indiana. ___ cm Last, he traveled from Indianapolis to St. Paul, Minnesota. ___ cm Diego started in...\nApproach: Choose the required unit, align equal units from the endpoint, measure without gaps or overlaps, and record the result with its unit.","Step 1: Identify what needs to be measured.\nStep 2: Choose the required unit and correct starting point.\nStep 3: Place or interpret equal-size units accurately.\nStep 4: Count or calculate the units and record the measure.\nStep 5: Check the endpoint, scale, and unit label.","Common mistake: using unequal units, leaving gaps, overlapping units, or starting at the wrong point. Check the unit and endpoints.","You will measure lengths in centimeters and add and subtract lengths within 20 to answer questions. In this lesson, you add and subtract within 20 and build fluency within this range of numbers."},new string[]{"IM2021 G2 Unit9 Lesson3: Measure on a Map PLC Activity. Verified public source-specific tasks and mathematics: Use ruler in centimeters on printed city-to-city Measurement Map, draw routes for named places and sum/difference the measured segments within20; students compare different lengths, NOT real-world miles or Grade6 map scales. Source media and human assessment boundary: Printed original Measurement Map distances depend on original geometry and ruler accuracy. Never invent a map measurement; originals and student constructed segments need teacher.","Focus: Use ruler in centimeters on printed city-to-city Measurement Map, draw routes for named places and sum/difference the measured segments within20; students compare different lengths, NOT real-world miles or Grade6 map scales. Standard mappings provided by ORIGINAL lesson graph: CCSS:2.MD.A.1,CCSS:2.MD.A.4,CCSS:2.MD.B.5,CCSS:2.OA.B.2. Original visual/physical question materials and authored open writing remain distinct from NEW Edulytics first-party numeric examples.","Actual public publisher activity: Use ruler in centimeters on printed city-to-city Measurement Map, draw routes for named places and sum/difference the measured segments within20; students compare different lengths, NOT real-world miles or Grade6 map scales. Independent first-party mathematical example uses different values and only exact numeric verification. Teacher separately reviews original representation or creative student problem and explanations: Printed original Measurement Map distances depend on original geometry and ruler accuracy. Never invent a map measurement; originals and student constructed segments need teacher.","Step 1 Read the source question and classify its actual given/unknown quantity, chosen representation, measured unit, or student-designed task. Step 2 For mental arithmetic use known within20 facts and make-ten strategies; for hundreds use place-value decompositions; for measurements use a correctly aligned centimeter ruler and never invent source segment lengths. Step 3 For stories, connect whole/part/comparison, order operations for one- and two-step problems, use tape diagram or number line only when it matches the text. Step 4 Solve FIRST-PARTY newly authored example with exact integer operations, check using the inverse or original equation and explain units. Step 5 Original cards/spinners/posters/images/pupil-created questions or physical measurement must receive human review. Source specifics: Use ruler in centimeters on printed city-to-city Measurement Map, draw routes for named places and sum/difference the measured segments within20; students compare different lengths, NOT real-world miles or Grade6 map scales.","Do not replace original student-selected card or spinner values with fabricated fixed results, assign numerical scores to personal strategy difficulty, conflate real-world map distance with measured centimeters, read a hidden photo count as fact, or treat a chosen story question as unique. Evidence limitation: Printed original Measurement Map distances depend on original geometry and ruler accuracy. Never invent a map measurement; originals and student constructed segments need teacher.","Original IM2021 G2 U09 L3: Use ruler in centimeters on printed city-to-city Measurement Map, draw routes for named places and sum/difference the measured segments within20; students compare different lengths, NOT real-world miles or Grade6 map scales. Separate Edulytics numeric practice checks one arithmetic skill but does not certify source classroom products. Printed original Measurement Map distances depend on original geometry and ruler accuracy. Never invent a map measurement; originals and student constructed segments need teacher."}),
new(137,"PED:US-CCSS-MATH:G2:U09:L04","https://im.kendallhunt.com/k5/teachers/grade-2/unit-9/lesson-4/preparation.html","39879a4c0f2c36d601282549dce30b6d37eb3fc19f757d10f3dd35eb87d55272",new string[]{"CCSS:2.MD.A.1","CCSS:2.MD.D.9","CCSS:2.NBT.B.5","CCSS:2.OA.B.2"},new string[]{"Learning goal: You will add and subtract within 20.\nIn this lesson, you continue to practice fluency with addition and subtraction within 20 with an emphasis on knowing from memory single-digit addition facts. You revisit line plots, which you worked with in a previous unit.","Key ideas:\n- Fluently add and subtract within 20\n- Measure lengths in centimeters\n- Represent measurement data in a line plot\n- Read category labels, values, and scales before comparing or calculating with a data display.","Example 1: What do you notice? What do you wonder? patient foot length (cm) A 12 B 18 C 20 D 18 E 18 F 20 G 17 H 21 Description: Line plot titled Length of Patient's Feet from 11 to 22&nbsp;by ones. Horizontal axis, length in centimeters.&nbsp;Beginning at 12, the number of X&rsquo;s above each number is 1, 0, 0, 0, 0, 1, 3, 0, 2, 1, 0.\nApproach: “How is the line plot the same as the table? How is it different?” (You both represent the lengths of patients’ feet. You both show the same measurements. The table helps you see the length of each patient’s foot. The line plot helps you see the measurements together, but doesn’t show you which patient had which length.)\nExample 2: group length of pencils in cm total length A 8 13 12 7 B 9 15 7 10 C 12 13 8 6 D 9 9 11 13 E Measure the length of your pencil. ___ cm Write the lengths of your group’s pencils in the table. Find the total length of each group’s pencils.","Step 1: Read the title, labels, categories, and scale.\nStep 2: Locate the values needed for the question.\nStep 3: Compare, add, subtract, or interpret the values as required.\nStep 4: State the result in the context of the data.","Common mistake: reading the wrong category or scale. Check the labels and scale before using the values.","You will add and subtract within 20. In this lesson, you continue to practice fluency with addition and subtraction within 20 with an emphasis on knowing from memory single-digit addition facts."},new string[]{"IM2021 G2 Unit9 Lesson4: Measure and Plot. Verified public source-specific tasks and mathematics: Original warmup foot-length table A=12,B=18,C=20,D=18,E=18,F=20,G=17,H=21 cm. Count actual original X marks; Activity1 physically measure own pencils and create class/group line plots, Activity2 compare recorded measures and lengths. Source media and human assessment boundary: Real pupil pencil lengths, original ruler zero alignment and students' completed line plots remain teacher-observed, not made-up fixed source measurements.","Focus: Original warmup foot-length table A=12,B=18,C=20,D=18,E=18,F=20,G=17,H=21 cm. Count actual original X marks; Activity1 physically measure own pencils and create class/group line plots, Activity2 compare recorded measures and lengths. Standard mappings provided by ORIGINAL lesson graph: CCSS:2.MD.A.1,CCSS:2.MD.D.9,CCSS:2.NBT.B.5,CCSS:2.OA.B.2. Original visual/physical question materials and authored open writing remain distinct from NEW Edulytics first-party numeric examples.","Actual public publisher activity: Original warmup foot-length table A=12,B=18,C=20,D=18,E=18,F=20,G=17,H=21 cm. Count actual original X marks; Activity1 physically measure own pencils and create class/group line plots, Activity2 compare recorded measures and lengths. Independent first-party mathematical example uses different values and only exact numeric verification. Teacher separately reviews original representation or creative student problem and explanations: Real pupil pencil lengths, original ruler zero alignment and students' completed line plots remain teacher-observed, not made-up fixed source measurements.","Step 1 Read the source question and classify its actual given/unknown quantity, chosen representation, measured unit, or student-designed task. Step 2 For mental arithmetic use known within20 facts and make-ten strategies; for hundreds use place-value decompositions; for measurements use a correctly aligned centimeter ruler and never invent source segment lengths. Step 3 For stories, connect whole/part/comparison, order operations for one- and two-step problems, use tape diagram or number line only when it matches the text. Step 4 Solve FIRST-PARTY newly authored example with exact integer operations, check using the inverse or original equation and explain units. Step 5 Original cards/spinners/posters/images/pupil-created questions or physical measurement must receive human review. Source specifics: Original warmup foot-length table A=12,B=18,C=20,D=18,E=18,F=20,G=17,H=21 cm. Count actual original X marks; Activity1 physically measure own pencils and create class/group line plots, Activity2 compare recorded measures and lengths.","Do not replace original student-selected card or spinner values with fabricated fixed results, assign numerical scores to personal strategy difficulty, conflate real-world map distance with measured centimeters, read a hidden photo count as fact, or treat a chosen story question as unique. Evidence limitation: Real pupil pencil lengths, original ruler zero alignment and students' completed line plots remain teacher-observed, not made-up fixed source measurements.","Original IM2021 G2 U09 L4: Original warmup foot-length table A=12,B=18,C=20,D=18,E=18,F=20,G=17,H=21 cm. Count actual original X marks; Activity1 physically measure own pencils and create class/group line plots, Activity2 compare recorded measures and lengths. Separate Edulytics numeric practice checks one arithmetic skill but does not certify source classroom products. Real pupil pencil lengths, original ruler zero alignment and students' completed line plots remain teacher-observed, not made-up fixed source measurements."}),
new(138,"PED:US-CCSS-MATH:G2:U09:L05","https://im.kendallhunt.com/k5/teachers/grade-2/unit-9/lesson-5/preparation.html","eadddc5d9f13e9433dc593a4b6a7cdc0a5b99e64e1b245a66fab459069ae4f25",new string[]{"CCSS:2.NBT.A.1"},new string[]{"Learning goal: You will compose and decompose three-digit numbers in different ways.\nIn previous units, you represented three-digit numbers by composing and decomposing units using base-ten blocks, base-ten diagrams, and equations. In this lesson, you compose and decompose three-digit numbers using base-ten blocks and other representations that make sense to them. Throughout the lesson, you are encouraged to show and deepen your understanding of place value by composing and decomposing units. - Action and Expression Activity 2: Let Me Count the Ways","Key ideas:\n- Compose and decompose numbers within 1,000\n- Represent numbers within 1,000 in different ways\n- Equivalent algebraic transformations preserve the represented relationship or solution set; check each transformation against the original.","Example 1: Start with 2 hundreds. Grab a handful of tens and of ones. What number do your base-ten blocks represent? ___ Represent the same number in another way. Show your thinking using diagrams, symbols, or other representations. Combine your blocks with your partner’s blocks. What number do your base-ten blocks represent? ___ Represent the same number in...\nApproach: “How could this group represent your number in another way?” (exchange 1 of the tens for 10 ones, exchange 10 tens for 1 hundred) “How could this group represent your number in another way?” (exchange 1 of the hundreds for 10 tens, exchange 1 ten for 10 ones, exchange all the tens for 40 ones)\nExample 2: Represent 356 in at least 3 different ways. Show your thinking using diagrams, symbols, or other representations. Create a poster with your group to show 356 in different ways.\nApproach: “Does this expression show 356? Explain.” (Yes. 16 ones is the same as one ten and 6 ones so it’s the same as 3 hundreds 5 tens and 6 ones.) “How are the expressions the same? How are you different?” (You all show 356. Some expressions have different amounts of hundreds and tens. Some expressions use words.)","Step 1: Identify what the task asks you to find or show.\nStep 2: Represent the important quantities or relationships.\nStep 3: Apply the key idea from the lesson.\nStep 4: Explain how the representation supports the result.\nStep 5: Check that the result matches the question.","Common mistake: jumping to an answer before checking that the representation matches the quantities and relationship in the task.","You will compose and decompose three-digit numbers in different ways. In previous units, you represented three-digit numbers by composing and decomposing units using base-ten blocks, base-ten diagrams, and equations."},new string[]{"IM2021 G2 Unit9 Lesson5: Compose and Decompose Numbers Within 1,000 PLC Activity. Verified public source-specific tasks and mathematics: Warm-up represent 308 in multiple valid ways. Original first activity start with 2 hundreds and random handful of tens/ones, combine actual partner blocks; no fixed source total. Activity2 represent 356 in three ways; 3 hundreds+4 tens+16 ones equals356 after composing a ten. Source media and human assessment boundary: Partner random block totals, actual posters, physical renaming moves and learner expressions are open human-evidence, not a unique forced original numeric answer.","Focus: Warm-up represent 308 in multiple valid ways. Original first activity start with 2 hundreds and random handful of tens/ones, combine actual partner blocks; no fixed source total. Activity2 represent 356 in three ways; 3 hundreds+4 tens+16 ones equals356 after composing a ten. Standard mappings provided by ORIGINAL lesson graph: CCSS:2.NBT.A.1. Original visual/physical question materials and authored open writing remain distinct from NEW Edulytics first-party numeric examples.","Actual public p