or treat a chosen story question as unique. Evidence limitation: Partner random block totals, actual posters, physical renaming moves and learner expressions are open human-evidence, not a unique forced original numeric answer.","Original IM2021 G2 U09 L5: Warm-up represent 308 in multiple valid ways. Original first activity start with 2 hundreds and random handful of tens/ones, combine actual partner blocks; no fixed source total. Activity2 represent 356 in three ways; 3 hundreds+4 tens+16 ones equals356 after composing a ten. Separate Edulytics numeric practice checks one arithmetic skill but does not certify source classroom products. Partner random block totals, actual posters, physical renaming moves and learner expressions are open human-evidence, not a unique forced original numeric answer."}),
new(139,"PED:US-CCSS-MATH:G2:U09:L06","https://im.kendallhunt.com/k5/teachers/grade-2/unit-9/lesson-6/preparation.html","b2a01addd694fd4bbbff6fc55751a8fdcb4e27c19a1a984638a210e306555a4f",new string[]{"CCSS:2.NBT.A.1","CCSS:2.NBT.A.3"},new string[]{"Learning goal: You will demonstrate your understanding of place value by composing and decomposing numbers within 1,000 in different ways.\nIn this lesson, you practice using place value reasoning to compose and decompose units to find unknown numbers and create equivalent forms of three-digit numbers. You find the unknown numbers that make expressions equivalent and match expressions that represent the same number. Although you should have access to base-ten blocks and tools to create base-ten diagrams as needed, you should also be encouraged to reason mentally based on your understanding of place value.","Key ideas:\n- Compose and decompose numbers within 1,000\n- Create and match expressions of numbers within 1,000\n- Choose a representation that matches the quantities and relationship, then use it to justify and check the result.","Example 1: Decide if each statement true or false. Be prepared to explain your reasoning. 5 hundreds + 2 tens + 7 ones = 527 4 hundreds + 12 tens + 7 ones = 527 5 hundreds + 7 ones + 2 tens = 527\nApproach: Represent the quantities or relationships clearly, apply the lesson idea, and check that the result answers the question.\nExample 2: Find the number that makes each equation true. 5 hundreds + ___ tens + 6 ones = 5 hundreds + 5 tens + 16 ones 1 hundred + 1 ten + 17 ones = 1 hundred + ___ tens + 7 ones 4 hundreds + 15 tens + 3 ones = ___ hundreds + 5 tens + 3 ones 4 hundreds + 3 tens + 7 ones = 3 hundreds + ___ tens + 7 ones 7 hundreds + 8 tens + 4 ones = 7 hundreds + 7 tens + ___ ones...\nApproach: Represent the quantities or relationships clearly, apply the lesson idea, and check that the result answers the question.","Step 1: Identify what the task asks you to find or show.\nStep 2: Represent the important quantities or relationships.\nStep 3: Apply the key idea from the lesson.\nStep 4: Explain how the representation supports the result.\nStep 5: Check that the result matches the question.","Common mistake: jumping to an answer before checking that the representation matches the quantities and relationship in the task.","You will demonstrate your understanding of place value by composing and decomposing numbers within 1,000 in different ways. In this lesson, you practice using place value reasoning to compose and decompose units to find unknown numbers and create equivalent forms of three-digit numbers."},new string[]{"IM2021 G2 Unit9 Lesson6: Represent Numbers with Expressions. Verified public source-specific tasks and mathematics: Original true/false 5 hundreds+2 tens+7 ones=527 true,4 hundreds+12 tens+7 ones=527 true,5 hundreds+7 ones+2 tens=527 true. Source original missing-place equations and printed card sort for same-value expressions, e.g. 5h+5t+16ones=566. Source media and human assessment boundary: Original printed cards labelled letters and student sort and equivalent-expression explanations require teacher; independently invented numbers are not original card answers.","Focus: Original true/false 5 hundreds+2 tens+7 ones=527 true,4 hundreds+12 tens+7 ones=527 true,5 hundreds+7 ones+2 tens=527 true. Source original missing-place equations and printed card sort for same-value expressions, e.g. 5h+5t+16ones=566. Standard mappings provided by ORIGINAL lesson graph: CCSS:2.NBT.A.1,CCSS:2.NBT.A.3. Original visual/physical question materials and authored open writing remain distinct from NEW Edulytics first-party numeric examples.","Actual public publisher activity: Original true/false 5 hundreds+2 tens+7 ones=527 true,4 hundreds+12 tens+7 ones=527 true,5 hundreds+7 ones+2 tens=527 true. Source original missing-place equations and printed card sort for same-value expressions, e.g. 5h+5t+16ones=566. Independent first-party mathematical example uses different values and only exact numeric verification. Teacher separately reviews original representation or creative student problem and explanations: Original printed cards labelled letters and student sort and equivalent-expression explanations require teacher; independently invented numbers are not original card answers.","Step 1 Read the source question and classify its actual given/unknown quantity, chosen representation, measured unit, or student-designed task. Step 2 For mental arithmetic use known within20 facts and make-ten strategies; for hundreds use place-value decompositions; for measurements use a correctly aligned centimeter ruler and never invent source segment lengths. Step 3 For stories, connect whole/part/comparison, order operations for one- and two-step problems, use tape diagram or number line only when it matches the text. Step 4 Solve FIRST-PARTY newly authored example with exact integer operations, check using the inverse or original equation and explain units. Step 5 Original cards/spinners/posters/images/pupil-created questions or physical measurement must receive human review. Source specifics: Original true/false 5 hundreds+2 tens+7 ones=527 true,4 hundreds+12 tens+7 ones=527 true,5 hundreds+7 ones+2 tens=527 true. Source original missing-place equations and printed card sort for same-value expressions, e.g. 5h+5t+16ones=566.","Do not replace original student-selected card or spinner values with fabricated fixed results, assign numerical scores to personal strategy difficulty, conflate real-world map distance with measured centimeters, read a hidden photo count as fact, or treat a chosen story question as unique. Evidence limitation: Original printed cards labelled letters and student sort and equivalent-expression explanations require teacher; independently invented numbers are not original card answers.","Original IM2021 G2 U09 L6: Original true/false 5 hundreds+2 tens+7 ones=527 true,4 hundreds+12 tens+7 ones=527 true,5 hundreds+7 ones+2 tens=527 true. Source original missing-place equations and printed card sort for same-value expressions, e.g. 5h+5t+16ones=566. Separate Edulytics numeric practice checks one arithmetic skill but does not certify source classroom products. Original printed cards labelled letters and student sort and equivalent-expression explanations require teacher; independently invented numbers are not original card answers."}),
new(140,"PED:US-CCSS-MATH:G2:U09:L07","https://im.kendallhunt.com/k5/teachers/grade-2/unit-9/lesson-7/preparation.html","014c75bdaadb875fca85d5c0ef3f48ea9ff9522937385ddb68643bd5185c6743",new string[]{"CCSS:2.NBT.B.5","CCSS:2.NBT.B.7"},new string[]{"Learning goal: You will add and subtract within 1,000 using methods based on place value.\nIn previous lessons, you practiced composing and decomposing three-digit numbers in different ways. The purpose of this lesson is to connect composing and decomposing numbers to methods for adding and subtracting numbers within 1,000. You are encouraged to think about what might make finding the values of some expressions more challenging.","Key ideas:\n- Add and subtract within 1,000 with and without regrouping\n- Fluently add and subtract within 100\n- Subtraction can represent taking away, comparing quantities, or finding a missing addend; the representation must match the situation.","Example 1: Which one doesn’t belong? \\(74 - 23\\) \\(24 + 37\\) 4 tens + 2 ones + 3 tens + 7 ones \\(60 + 19\\)\nApproach: Represent the whole and the part, write a matching subtraction relationship, then find and check the difference.\nExample 2: A \\(102 + 341\\) G \\(64 + 37\\) B \\(385 - 124\\) H \\(302 - 198\\) C \\(611 - 45\\) I \\(73 - 27\\) D \\(87 + 38\\) J \\(685 + 279\\) E \\(571 - 268\\) K \\(320 + 509\\) F \\(885 - 142\\) L \\(53 + 52\\) A \\(102 + 341\\) G \\(64 + 37\\) B \\(385 - 124\\) H \\(302-198\\) C \\(611 - 45\\) I \\(73 - 27\\) D \\(87 + 38\\) J \\(685 + 279\\) E \\(571 - 268\\) K \\(320 + 509\\) F \\(885 - 142\\) L \\(53...\nApproach: Represent the whole and the part, write a matching subtraction relationship, then find and check the difference.","Step 1: Identify the whole and the part being removed or compared.\nStep 2: Represent the situation with objects, a drawing, number line, or equation.\nStep 3: Write a subtraction relationship that matches the representation.\nStep 4: Find the difference using an efficient strategy.\nStep 5: Check the result with the original quantities or a related addition fact.","Common mistake: mixing up the whole and the part being removed or compared. Check that the subtraction relationship matches the representation.","You will add and subtract within 1,000 using methods based on place value. In previous lessons, you practiced composing and decomposing three-digit numbers in different ways."},new string[]{"IM2021 G2 Unit9 Lesson7: Add and Subtract Within 1,000. Verified public source-specific tasks and mathematics: Warm-up source Which One Doesn't Belong: 74−23=51,24+37=61, four tens+two ones+three tens+seven ones=79,60+19=79. Original Activity1 compare perceived difficulty across labelled A–L expressions including A102+341=443, B385−124=261, H302−198=104 and J685+279=964; optional choice of method valid. Activity2 fluency within100. Source media and human assessment boundary: Student ratings of challenge, selected sets, independent representations and chosen alternative valid strategies are not uniquely auto-graduated.","Focus: Warm-up source Which One Doesn't Belong: 74−23=51,24+37=61, four tens+two ones+three tens+seven ones=79,60+19=79. Original Activity1 compare perceived difficulty across labelled A–L expressions including A102+341=443, B385−124=261, H302−198=104 and J685+279=964; optional choice of method valid. Activity2 fluency within100. Standard mappings provided by ORIGINAL lesson graph: CCSS:2.NBT.B.5,CCSS:2.NBT.B.7. Original visual/physical question materials and authored open writing remain distinct from NEW Edulytics first-party numeric examples.","Actual public publisher activity: Warm-up source Which One Doesn't Belong: 74−23=51,24+37=61, four tens+two ones+three tens+seven ones=79,60+19=79. Original Activity1 compare perceived difficulty across labelled A–L expressions including A102+341=443, B385−124=261, H302−198=104 and J685+279=964; optional choice of method valid. Activity2 fluency within100. Independent first-party mathematical