our tens+two ones+three tens+seven ones=79,60+19=79. Original Activity1 compare perceived difficulty across labelled A–L expressions including A102+341=443, B385−124=261, H302−198=104 and J685+279=964; optional choice of method valid. Activity2 fluency within100. Separate Edulytics numeric practice checks one arithmetic skill but does not certify source classroom products. Student ratings of challenge, selected sets, independent representations and chosen alternative valid strategies are not uniquely auto-graduated."}),
new(141,"PED:US-CCSS-MATH:G2:U09:L08","https://im.kendallhunt.com/k5/teachers/grade-2/unit-9/lesson-8/preparation.html","5f6d35dffa7a83a1c0a93391728e75fe2d46806f0e7b2fda7c551bbc02f3c416",new string[]{"CCSS:2.NBT.B.5","CCSS:2.NBT.B.7"},new string[]{"Learning goal: You will practice addition and subtraction within 100 to build fluency.\nIn previous lessons, you composed and decomposed units to add and subtract within 1,000. You considered which sums and differences within 100 were more or less difficult for them to find. In this lesson, you develop fluency with adding and subtracting within 100.","Key ideas:\n- Add and subtract within 100\n- In previous lessons, you composed and decomposed units to add and subtract within 1,000\n- Subtraction can represent taking away, comparing quantities, or finding a missing addend; the representation must match the situation.","Example 1: Find the value of each expression mentally. \\(9 + 5\\) \\(20 + 30\\) \\(29 + 35\\) \\(229 + 435\\)\nApproach: “How is each sum related to the others?” (Sample response: The third expression is like the first and the second because you have to add \\(9+5\\) and \\(20+30\\) if you add by place.)\nExample 2: Write an equation that shows the value you found for each round you play. Round 1: Round 2: Round 3:\nApproach: “Which role did you like best—Player C or Player A and B? Explain.” (I liked to be Player C because I like to add. I liked being Player A or B because I could add or subtract. I liked being Player A or B because I could practice subtracting.)","Step 1: Identify the whole and the part being removed or compared.\nStep 2: Represent the situation with objects, a drawing, number line, or equation.\nStep 3: Write a subtraction relationship that matches the representation.\nStep 4: Find the difference using an efficient strategy.\nStep 5: Check the result with the original quantities or a related addition fact.","Common mistake: mixing up the whole and the part being removed or compared. Check that the subtraction relationship matches the representation.","You will practice addition and subtraction within 100 to build fluency. In previous lessons, you composed and decomposed units to add and subtract within 1,000."},new string[]{"IM2021 G2 Unit9 Lesson8: Add and Subtract Within 100. Verified public source-specific tasks and mathematics: Warm-up source 9+5=14,20+30=50,29+35=64,229+435=664; source Heads Up cards and original Five in a Row Stage6 or Target Numbers Stages3–5 optional center choices; fluency add/sub within100. Source media and human assessment boundary: Original real card values, heads-up roles, paper center board, student preferences and outcomes teacher observed, never attributed to first-party check.","Focus: Warm-up source 9+5=14,20+30=50,29+35=64,229+435=664; source Heads Up cards and original Five in a Row Stage6 or Target Numbers Stages3–5 optional center choices; fluency add/sub within100. Standard mappings provided by ORIGINAL lesson graph: CCSS:2.NBT.B.5,CCSS:2.NBT.B.7. Original visual/physical question materials and authored open writing remain distinct from NEW Edulytics first-party numeric examples.","Actual public publisher activity: Warm-up source 9+5=14,20+30=50,29+35=64,229+435=664; source Heads Up cards and original Five in a Row Stage6 or Target Numbers Stages3–5 optional center choices; fluency add/sub within100. Independent first-party mathematical example uses different values and only exact numeric verification. Teacher separately reviews original representation or creative student problem and explanations: Original real card values, heads-up roles, paper center board, student preferences and outcomes teacher observed, never attributed to first-party check.","Step 1 Read the source question and classify its actual given/unknown quantity, chosen representation, measured unit, or student-designed task. Step 2 For mental arithmetic use known within20 facts and make-ten strategies; for hundreds use place-value decompositions; for measurements use a correctly aligned centimeter ruler and never invent source segment lengths. Step 3 For stories, connect whole/part/comparison, order operations for one- and two-step problems, use tape diagram or number line only when it matches the text. Step 4 Solve FIRST-PARTY newly authored example with exact integer operations, check using the inverse or original equation and explain units. Step 5 Original cards/spinners/posters/images/pupil-created questions or physical measurement must receive human review. Source specifics: Warm-up source 9+5=14,20+30=50,29+35=64,229+435=664; source Heads Up cards and original Five in a Row Stage6 or Target Numbers Stages3–5 optional center choices; fluency add/sub within100.","Do not replace original student-selected card or spinner values with fabricated fixed results, assign numerical scores to personal strategy difficulty, conflate real-world map distance with measured centimeters, read a hidden photo count as fact, or treat a chosen story question as unique. Evidence limitation: Original real card values, heads-up roles, paper center board, student preferences and outcomes teacher observed, never attributed to first-party check.","Original IM2021 G2 U09 L8: Warm-up source 9+5=14,20+30=50,29+35=64,229+435=664; source Heads Up cards and original Five in a Row Stage6 or Target Numbers Stages3–5 optional center choices; fluency add/sub within100. Separate Edulytics numeric practice checks one arithmetic skill but does not certify source classroom products. Original real card values, heads-up roles, paper center board, student preferences and outcomes teacher observed, never attributed to first-party check."}),
new(142,"PED:US-CCSS-MATH:G2:U09:L09","https://im.kendallhunt.com/k5/teachers/grade-2/unit-9/lesson-9/preparation.html","f98057f3c3fa8bf15d987df9251191a0d6ac5d962b08fcdf91d4139ef2581c4b",new string[]{"CCSS:2.NBT.B.5","CCSS:2.NBT.B.9","CCSS:2.OA.A.1"},new string[]{"Learning goal: You will compare, connect, and solve story problems.\nIn previous lessons, you solved all the types of story problems including Add To, Take From, Put Together, and Compare with the unknown in all positions. The purpose of this lesson is to sort a variety of story problems, including two-step problems, and choose a problem to solve. You sort the story problems and describe the categories you create.","Key ideas:\n- Analyze story problems involving addition and subtraction within 100\n- In previous lessons, you solved all the types of story problems including Add To, Take From, Put Together, and Compare with the unknown in all positions\n- The addends are the quantities being combined and the sum is the resulting total; the expression must match the representation.","Example 1: Find the value of each expression mentally. \\(10 - 6\\) \\(14 - 6\\) \\(54 - 6\\) \\(54 - 26\\)\nApproach: “How can you use the result of \\(14 - 6\\) to find the value of \\(54 - 6\\) ?” (54 has 4 more tens than 14 so add 4 tens or 40 to the result of \\(14 - 6\\) .) “How can you use the result of \\(54 - 6\\) to find the value of \\(54 - 26\\) ?” (26 has 2 more tens than 6 so that means 2 tens need to be taken away from the answer to \\(54 - 6\\) .)\nExample 2: Sort the story problems in a way that makes sense to you. Jada has some coins with a value of 92 cents. She gives Andre a dime and a quarter. How many cents does Jada have now? 2. Tyler put 15 apples in the empty basket. Lin put 18 apples in the basket. Noah took 7 apples out of the basket. How many apples are in the basket now? 3. On Monday, 47 cm of...\nApproach: Represent both quantities, compare their values, and state the relationship using words, symbols, or a clear explanation.","Step 1: Identify the quantities or expressions to compare.\nStep 2: Represent or calculate each value accurately.\nStep 3: Decide whether one is greater, less, or equal.\nStep 4: Write or explain the comparison.\nStep 5: Recheck both values before finalizing.","Common mistake: deciding from appearance instead of actual values. Represent or calculate both quantities before comparing.","You will compare, connect, and solve story problems. In previous lessons, you solved all the types of story problems including Add To, Take From, Put Together, and Compare with the unknown in all positions."},new string[]{"IM2021 G2 Unit9 Lesson9: Sort the Story Problems. Verified public source-specific tasks and mathematics: Warm-up 10−6=4,14−6=8,54−6=48,54−26=28. Original story card sort (add to, take from, put together, compare, one/two-step, unknown position): Jada 92¢ gives 10¢ and 25¢ →57¢; Tyler15 apples+Lin18−Noah7→26. Pupils choose categories and one source problem to solve, compare their reasoning. Source media and human assessment boundary: No single prescribed sorting group is mathematically mandatory; source story cards, authentic student sorting rationale and tape diagrams need educator.","Focus: Warm-up 10−6=4,14−6=8,54−6=48,54−26=28. Original story card sort (add to, take from, put together, compare, one/two-step, unknown position): Jada 92¢ gives 10¢ and 25¢ →57¢; Tyler15 apples+Lin18−Noah7→26. Pupils choose categories and one source problem to solve, compare their reasoning. Standard mappings provided by ORIGINAL lesson graph: CCSS:2.NBT.B.5,CCSS:2.NBT.B.9,CCSS:2.OA.A.1. Original visual/physical question materials and authored open writing remain distinct from NEW Edulytics first-party numeric examples.","Actual public publisher activity: Warm-up 10−6=4,14−6=8,54−6=48,54−26=28. Original story card sort (add to, take from, put together, compare, one/two-step, unknown position): Jada 92¢ gives 10¢ and 25¢ →57¢; Tyler15 apples+Lin18−Noah7→26.