cator.","Original IM2021 G2 U09 L9: Warm-up 10−6=4,14−6=8,54−6=48,54−26=28. Original story card sort (add to, take from, put together, compare, one/two-step, unknown position): Jada 92¢ gives 10¢ and 25¢ →57¢; Tyler15 apples+Lin18−Noah7→26. Pupils choose categories and one source problem to solve, compare their reasoning. Separate Edulytics numeric practice checks one arithmetic skill but does not certify source classroom products. No single prescribed sorting group is mathematically mandatory; source story cards, authentic student sorting rationale and tape diagrams need educator."}),
new(143,"PED:US-CCSS-MATH:G2:U09:L10","https://im.kendallhunt.com/k5/teachers/grade-2/unit-9/lesson-10/preparation.html","4852071d4ef4e24610dff7ceaa8225b8b27d3a02773987226234ac049372e253",new string[]{"CCSS:2.NBT.B.5","CCSS:2.OA.A.1"},new string[]{"Learning goal: You will analyze a tape diagram and number line diagram and determine, in context, a question that could be answered based on each representation.\nIn previous lessons, you sorted and solved a variety of story problems. In this lesson, you use given information to ask math questions and figure out what question was asked when presented with student work. You interpret the context of a story and analyze tape diagrams to determine what question is being asked (MP2, MP4).","Key ideas:\n- Ask and answer a question based on a given situation\n- Interpret story problems using diagrams\n- Choose a representation that matches the quantities and relationship, then use it to justify and check the result.","Example 1: In the park, there are 37 kids on the soccer field, 18 kids on the tennis courts, and 25 kids at the picnic tables. What do you notice? What do you wonder?\nApproach: Represent the quantities or relationships clearly, apply the lesson idea, and check that the result answers the question.\nExample 2: Elena picked 29 apples. Elena picked 14 fewer apples than Han. Han picked 15 more apples than Diego. Write a question someone could answer based on this information. Answer your question. Show your thinking using diagrams, symbols, or other representations.\nApproach: “What does the diagram represent?” (The number of apples Han picked since he picked 14 more than Elena.) “How did you find how many apples Han picked?” (I added 30 to 14 to get 44 and then took away 1 to get 43.) “How did you find how many apples Diego picked?” (I took 15 away from 43.) “How did you find how many apples Elena, Han, and Diego picked altogether?” (I added 29, 43, and 28. I put all the tens together...","Step 1: Identify what the task asks you to find or show.\nStep 2: Represent the important quantities or relationships.\nStep 3: Apply the key idea from the lesson.\nStep 4: Explain how the representation supports the result.\nStep 5: Check that the result matches the question.","Common mistake: jumping to an answer before checking that the representation matches the quantities and relationship in the task.","You will analyze a tape diagram and number line diagram and determine, in context, a question that could be answered based on each representation. In previous lessons, you sorted and solved a variety of story problems."},new string[]{"IM2021 G2 Unit9 Lesson10: What’s the Question? PLC Activity. Verified public source-specific tasks and mathematics: Source park given 37 soccer,18 tennis,25 picnic. Learner MUST WRITE a suitable mathematical question before solving, e.g. all three total80, difference soccer-tennis19. Source apple story Elena29, Elena14 fewer than Han→Han43, Han15 more than Diego→Diego28, combined100. Original tape diagrams and question choice human. Source media and human assessment boundary: A numerical answer without asking a coherent question or respecting the chosen unknown is not authentic source success; student questions/drawings require teacher.","Focus: Source park given 37 soccer,18 tennis,25 picnic. Learner MUST WRITE a suitable mathematical question before solving, e.g. all three total80, difference soccer-tennis19. Source apple story Elena29, Elena14 fewer than Han→Han43, Han15 more than Diego→Diego28, combined100. Original tape diagrams and question choice human. Standard mappings provided by ORIGINAL lesson graph: CCSS:2.NBT.B.5,CCSS:2.OA.A.1. Original visual/physical question materials and authored open writing remain distinct from NEW Edulytics first-party numeric examples.","Actual public publisher activity: Source park given 37 soccer,18 tennis,25 picnic. Learner MUST WRITE a suitable mathematical question before solving, e.g. all three total80, difference