er without asking a coherent question or respecting the chosen unknown is not authentic source success; student questions/drawings require teacher.","Original IM2021 G2 U09 L10: Source park given 37 soccer,18 tennis,25 picnic. Learner MUST WRITE a suitable mathematical question before solving, e.g. all three total80, difference soccer-tennis19. Source apple story Elena29, Elena14 fewer than Han→Han43, Han15 more than Diego→Diego28, combined100. Original tape diagrams and question choice human. Separate Edulytics numeric practice checks one arithmetic skill but does not certify source classroom products. A numerical answer without asking a coherent question or respecting the chosen unknown is not authentic source success; student questions/drawings require teacher."}),
new(144,"PED:US-CCSS-MATH:G2:U09:L11","https://im.kendallhunt.com/k5/teachers/grade-2/unit-9/lesson-11/preparation.html","a9cd3e8f1ae6a0d9c2a7053216464815e1572fa49ff3edab2db8a61f43e0d8dd",new string[]{"CCSS:2.NBT.B.5","CCSS:2.OA.A.1"},new string[]{"Learning goal: You will make connections between story problems, equations, and tape diagrams.\nIn previous lessons, you analyzed and solved story problems. In this lesson, you match story problems with tape diagrams and equations and write stories based on a tape diagram (MP2). The words in each story need to be interpreted carefully in order to decide which equation is the best match.","Key ideas:\n- Write and interpret story problems using diagrams and equations\n- In previous lessons, you analyzed and solved story problems\n- Equivalent algebraic transformations preserve the represented relationship or solution set; check each transformation against the original.","Example 1: Find the value of each expression mentally. \\(7 + 13\\) \\(27 + 13\\) \\(40 - 13\\) \\(40 - 27\\)\nApproach: “How can you use your answer for \\(27 + 13\\) to find the value of \\(40 - 13\\) ?” ( \\(27 + 13\\) is 40 so \\(40 - 13\\) is 27.) “How can you use your answer for \\(27 + 13\\) to find the value of \\(40 - 27\\) ?” ( \\(27 + 13\\) is 40 so \\(40 - 27\\) is 13.)\nExample 2: Match the stories, diagrams, and equations.\nApproach: “How are the two diagrams the same? How are you different?” (Both diagrams have a 53 and a 25. One diagram shows two separate rectangles comparing quantities. 53 is the total for one of the rectangles. In the other diagram 53 is one part and the total is unknown.) “Which problem does the first diagram match? How do you know?” (The baseball and tennis ball problem. The first bar shows 53 and the second bar shows...","Step 1: Identify what the task asks you to find or show.\nStep 2: Represent the important quantities or relationships.\nStep 3: Apply the key idea from the lesson.\nStep 4: Explain how the representation supports the result.\nStep 5: Check that the result matches the question.","Common mistake: jumping to an answer before checking that the representation matches the quantities and relationship in the task.","You will make connections between story problems, equations, and tape diagrams. In previous lessons, you analyzed and solved story problems."},new string[]{"IM2021 G2 Unit9 Lesson11: All About Tape Diagrams. Verified public source-specific tasks and mathematics: Warm-up 7+13=20,27+13=40,40−13=27,40−27=13. Match actual source tape diagrams, equations and story cards. Distinguish 53 as known whole versus 53 as part, 25 as quantity removed versus 25 difference; construct own story for printed diagram. Source media and human assessment boundary: Matching ORIGINAL diagram artwork and student-made story or line diagram require teacher. Numeric firstparty subset only cannot certify visual match.","Focus: Warm-up 7+13=20,27+13=40,40−13=27,40−27=13. Match actual source tape diagrams, equations and story cards. Distinguish 53 as known whole versus 53 as part, 25 as quantity removed versus 25 difference; construct own story for printed diagram. Standard mappings provided by ORIGINAL lesson graph: CCSS:2.NBT.B.5,CCSS:2.OA.A.1. Original visual/physical question materials and authored open writing remain distinct from NEW Edulytics first-party numeric examples.","Actual public publisher activity: Warm-up 7+13=20,27+13=40,40−13=27,40−27=13. Match actual source tape diagrams, equations and story cards. Distinguish 53 as known whole versus 53 as part, 25 as quantity removed versus 25 difference; construct own story for printed diagram. Independent first-party mathematical example uses different values and only exact numeric verification. Teacher separately reviews original representation or creative student problem and explanations: Matching ORIGINAL diagram artwork and student-made story or line diagram require teacher. Numeric firstparty subset only cannot certify visual match.","Step 1 Read the source question and classify its actual given/unknown quantity, chosen representation, measured unit, or student-designed task. Step 2 For mental arithmetic use known within20 facts and make-ten strategies; for hundreds use place-value decompositions; for measurements use a correctly aligned centimeter ruler and never invent source segment lengths. Step 3 For stories, connect whole/part/comparison, order operations for one- and two-step problems, use tape diagram or number line only when it matches the text. Step 4 Solve FIRST-PARTY newly authored example with exact integer operations, check using the inverse or original equation and explain units. Step 5 Original cards/spinners/posters/images/pupil-created questions or physical measurement must receive human review. Source specifics: Warm-up 7+13=20,27+13=40,40−13=27,40−27=13. Match actual source tape diagrams, equations and story cards. Distinguish 53 as known whole versus 53 as part, 25 as quantity removed versus 25 difference; construct own story for printed diagram.","Do not replace original student-selected card or spinner values with fabricated fixed results, assign numerical scores to personal strategy difficulty, conflate real-world map distance with measured centimeters, read a hidden photo count as fact, or treat a chosen story question as unique. Evidence limitation: Matching ORIGINAL diagram artwork and student-made story or line diagram require teacher. Numeric firstparty subset only cannot certify visual match.","Original IM2021 G2 U09 L11: Warm-up 7+13=20,27+13=40,40−13=27,40−27=13. Match actual source tape diagrams, equations and story cards. Distinguish 53 as known whole versus 53 as part, 25 as quantity removed versus 25 difference; construct own story for printed diagram. Separate Edulytics numeric practice checks one arithmetic skill but does not certify source classroom products. Matching ORIGINAL diagram artwork and student-made story or line diagram require teacher. Numeric firstparty subset only cannot certify visual match."}),
new(145,"PED:US-CCSS-MATH:G2:U09:L12","https://im.kendallhunt.com/k5/teachers/grade-2/unit-9/lesson-12/preparation.html","3d8f619b82e233f6d61276aaf5154f0aa416685ba9e153b8121bf9f8b5ad60ef",new string[]{"CCSS:2.OA.A.1"},new string[]{"Learning goal: You will ask mathematical questions and write story problems based on a given expression.\nIn previous lessons, you solved story problems of all types, matched them with tape diagrams and equations, and wrote stories to match tape diagrams. In this lesson, you write story problems to match equations, ask mathematical questions based on pictures and about your environment, and write story problems based on your observations.","Key ideas:\n- See math in the world around them\n- Write story problems using expressions\n- Equivalent algebraic transformations preserve the represented relationship or solution set; check each transformation against the original.","Example 1: How many people are in the picture? Attribution: By Airman 1st Class Stormy Archer. Public Domain. Joint Base San Antonio. Source . Record an estimate that is: too low about right too high \\(\\phantom{\\hspace{2.5cm} \\\\ \\hspace{2.5cm}}\\) \\(\\phantom{\\hspace{2.5cm} \\\\ \\hspace{2.5cm}}\\) \\(\\phantom{\\hspace{2.5cm} \\\\ \\hspace{2.5cm}}\\)\nApproach: “How did you make an estimate that was too low?” (I saw 10 to 20 kids running in front and then a whole lot of people behind them, so I made a low estimate of 50.) “How did you make an estimate that was too high?” (It is hard to tell how far back the people go, so I just said 1,000.)\nExample 2: Write a story problem.\nApproach: Identify the quantities and relationships, represent them with expressions or equations, solve or transform systematically, and check the result in the original relationship.","Step 1: Identify the unknowns and the relationships between quantities.\nStep 2: Translate the relationships into expressions, equations, graphs, or tables.\nStep 3: Apply valid algebraic operations or transformations.\nStep 4: Solve or interpret the result.\nStep 5: Check the result in the original equation, representation, or context.","Common mistake: changing an expression or equation without applying a valid operation consistently. Check each transformation and substitute back when possible.","You will ask mathematical questions and write story problems based on a given expression. In previous lessons, you solved story problems of all types, matched them with tape diagrams and equations, and wrote stories to match tape diagrams."},new string[]{"IM2021 G2 Unit9 Lesson12: What’s the Story?. Verified public source-specific tasks and mathematics: Warm-up original photo 5K runners, estimate a too-small/about-right/too-high COUNT with uncertainty; NOT a precise source total. Activity1 authors story problems matching two given original equations (unknown may be start) and Activity2 math walk: choose own countable scene, write and exchange math stories. Source media and human assessment boundary: Photo total unknown, plausible estimate range is not unique, original student context and partner story validity require educator. No fabricated official race count.","Focus: Warm-up original photo 5K runners, estimate a too-small/about-right/too-high COUNT with uncertainty; NOT a precise source total. Activity1 authors story problems matching two given original equations (unknown may be start) and Activity2 math walk: choose own countable scene, write and exchange math stories. Standard mappings provided by ORIGINAL lesson graph: CCSS:2.OA.A.1. Original visual/physical question materials and authored open writing remain distinct from NEW Edulytics first-party numeric examples.","Actual public publisher activity: Warm-up original photo 5K runners, estimate a too-small/about-right/too-high COUNT with uncertainty; NOT a precise source total. Activity1 authors story problems matching two given original equations (unknown may be start) and Activity2 math walk: choose own countable scene, write and exchange math stories. Independent first-party mathematical example uses different values and only exact numeric verification. Teacher separately reviews original representation or creative student problem and explanations: Photo total unknown, plausible estimate range is not unique, original student context and partner story validity require educator. No fabricated official race count.","Step 1 Read the source question and classify its actual given/unknown quantity, chosen representation, measured unit, or student-designed task. Step 2 For mental arithmetic use known within20 facts and make-ten strategies; for hundreds use place-value decompositions; for measurements use a correctly aligned centimeter ruler and never invent source segment lengths. Step 3 For stories, connect whole/part/comparison, order operations for one- and two-step problems, use tape diagram or number line only when it matches the text. Step 4 Solve FIRST-PARTY newly authored example with exact integer operations, check using the inverse or original equation and explain units. Step 5 Original cards/spinners/posters/images/pupil-created questions or physical measurement must receive human review. Source specifics: Warm-up original photo 5K runners, estimate a too-small/about-right/too-high COUNT with uncertainty; NOT a precise source total. Activity1 authors story problems matching two given original equations (unknown may be start) and Activity2 math walk: choose own countable scene, write and exchange math stories.","Do not replace original student-selected card or spinner values with fabricated fixed results, assign numerical scores to personal strategy difficulty, conflate real-world map distance with measured centimeters, read a hidden photo count as fact, or treat a chosen story question as unique. Evidence limitation: Photo total unknown, plausible estimate range is not unique, original student context and partner story validity require educator. No fabricated official race count.","Original IM2021 G2 U09 L12: Warm-up original photo 5K runners, estimate a too-small/about-right/too-high COUNT with uncertainty; NOT a precise source total. Activity1 authors story problems matching two given original equations (unknown may be start) and Activity2 math walk: choose own countable scene, write and exchange math stories. Separate Edulytics numeric practice checks one arithmetic skill but does not certify source classroom products. Photo total unknown, plausible estimate range is not unique, original student context and partner story validity require educator. No fabricated official race count."}),
new(146,"PED:US-CCSS-MATH:G2:U09:L13","https://im.kendallhunt.com/k5/teachers/grade-2/unit-9/lesson-13/preparation.html","d06a0120de3209c2c81684edfe7db8065fff36692067056ed038ba3456358a9e",new string[]{"CCSS:2.NBT.B.5","CCSS:2.OA.A.1"},new string[]{"Learning goal: You will solve story problems and represent your thinking.\nIn the previous lesson, you created stories based on your observations. In this lesson, you solve your story problems and represent your work for others to see. You take a gallery walk to see and compare the different stories and representations.","Key ideas:\n- Represent and solve story problems\n- In the previous lesson, you created stories based on your observations\n- Compare actual values or measured attributes, not the visual size or position of a drawing.","Example 1: Find the value of each expression mentally. \\(38 + 7\\) \\(38 + 17\\) \\(38 + 27\\) \\(38 + 57\\)\nApproach: “How does this help you calculate the third and fourth sums?” (I just add some more tens.)\nExample 2: Solve the story problem you wrote yesterday. Make a poster of your story problem. Make sure to include: your story with the question your thinking and reasoning to solve the problem, using: pictures diagrams words expressions the answer to your story problem question\nApproach: “What are some things that you will look for when you look at the posters?” (Do I understand the story? Do I agree with the solution? Can I follow the thinking or reasoning?) “What are some different ways you can show solutions to the problems?” (pictures of objects, base-ten pictures, diagrams, number line diagrams, expressions, equations)","Step 1: Identify what the task asks you to find or show.\nStep 2: Represent the important quantities or relationships.\nStep 3: Apply the key idea from the lesson.\nStep 4: Explain how the representation supports the result.\nStep 5: Check that the result matches the question.","Common mistake: jumping to an answer before checking that the representation matches the quantities and relationship in the task.","You will solve story problems and represent your thinking. In the previous lesson, you created stories based on your observations."},new string[]{"IM2021 G2 Unit9 Lesson13: Let’s Solve Our Story Problems. Verified public source-specific tasks and mathematics: Warm-up 38+7=45,38+17=55,38+27=65,38+57=95. Students solve THEIR OWN stories from preceding lesson, make poster containing scenario, question, worked diagrams/words/equations and numeric answer, then gallery walk compares reasoning. Source media and human assessment boundary: Original student stories, diagrams, valid mathematical model, class gallery feedback and absent source-specific poster cannot be auto-certified or invented.","Focus: Warm-up 38+7=45,38+17=55,38+27=65,38+57=95. Students solve THEIR OWN stories from preceding lesson, make poster containing scenario, question, worked diagrams/words/equations and numeric answer, then gallery walk compares reasoning. Standard mappings provided by ORIGINAL lesson graph: CCSS:2.NBT.B.5,CCSS:2.OA.A.1. Original visual/physical question materials and authored open writing remain distinct from NEW Edulytics first-party numeric examples.","Actual public publisher activity: Warm-up 38+7=45,38+17=55,38+27=65,38+57=95. Students solve THEIR OWN stories from preceding lesson, make poster containing scenario, question, worked diagrams/words/equations and numeric answer, then gallery walk compares reasoning. Independent first-party mathematical example uses different values and only exact numeric verification. Teacher separately reviews original representation or creative student problem and explanations: Original student stories, diagrams, valid mathematical model, class gallery feedback and absent source-specific poster cannot be auto-certified or invented.","Step 1 Read the source question and classify its actual given/unknown quantity, chosen representation, measured unit, or student-designed task. Step 2 For mental arithmetic use known within20 facts and make-ten strategies; for hundreds use place-value decompositions; for measurements use a correctly aligned centimeter ruler and never invent source segment lengths. Step 3 For stories, connect whole/part/comparison, order operations for one- and two-step problems, use tape diagram or number line only when it matches the text. Step 4 Solve FIRST-PARTY newly authored example with exact integer operations, check using the inverse or original equation and explain units. Step 5 Original cards/spinners/posters/images/pupil-created questions or physical measurement must receive human review. Source specifics: Warm-up 38+7=45,38+17=55,38+27=65,38+57=95. Students solve THEIR OWN stories from preceding lesson, make poster containing scenario, question, worked diagrams/words/equations and numeric answer, then gallery walk compares reasoning.","Do not replace original student-selected card or spinner values with fabricated fixed results, assign numerical scores to personal strategy difficulty, conflate real-world map distance with measured centimeters, read a hidden photo count as fact, or treat a chosen story question as unique. Evidence limitation: Original student stories, diagrams, valid mathematical model, class gallery feedback and absent source-specific poster cannot be auto-certified or invented.","Original IM2021 G2 U09 L13: Warm-up 38+7=45,38+17=55,38+27=65,38+57=95. Students solve THEIR OWN stories from preceding lesson, make poster containing scenario, question, worked diagrams/words/equations and numeric answer, then gallery walk compares reasoning. Separate Edulytics numeric practice checks one arithmetic skill but does not certify source classroom products. Original student stories, diagrams, valid mathematical model, class gallery feedback and absent source-specific poster cannot be auto-certified or invented."})
